1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,584
Only two percent of developers get this Kafka question right.

2
00:00:04,534 --> 00:00:11,500
If your Kafka producer sets acks=0 and the broker crashes, what happens to your message?

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:13,988
Take a moment to think about it.

4
00:00:13,988 --> 00:00:17,340
Here is what most developers get wrong.

5
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:19,613
Kafka producer config.

6
00:00:19,613 --> 00:00:21,295
Look at this.

7
00:00:21,295 --> 00:00:24,102
The answer is B — gone forever.

8
00:00:24,102 --> 00:00:27,897
acks=0 means fire and forget.

9
00:00:27,897 --> 00:00:31,761
Your producer does not even wait for confirmation.

10
00:00:31,761 --> 00:00:37,181
Most production systems lose data this way and do not know for months.

11
00:00:37,181 --> 00:00:44,102
LinkedIn processes seven trillion messages per day and every single one uses acks=all.

12
00:00:44,102 --> 00:00:48,124
Let me show you a real example of this in production.

13
00:00:48,124 --> 00:00:53,409
One config line is the difference between data safety and data loss.

14
00:00:53,409 --> 00:00:56,670
But here is the part nobody talks about.

15
00:00:56,670 --> 00:00:58,295
The scary part?

16
00:00:58,295 --> 00:01:02,034
acks=0 is not even the default.

17
00:01:02,034 --> 00:01:08,590
acks=1 is — and that is ALSO unsafe if the leader crashes before replication.

